  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Heat Advisory in effect for Virginia Piedmont.\n\nConditions this afternoon will be unusually hot for this time of \nyear. High temperatures are expected to be from the mid-90s to lower \n100s east of the Blue Ridge. With dew points in the mid-60s to lower \n70s, heat index values will likely get to at least 105 degrees in \nthe Virginia Piedmont. A Heat Advisory has been issued to reflect \nthis hazard. Cloud cover from storms in eastern Virginia later today \nmay be able to prevent the worst of it but heat related \nillnesses will still be possible if the public does not hydrate \noften and stay in cooled areas.\n\nThursday is still expected to be the hottest part of the week with \nthe center of the mid-level ridge closest to us. While subtle \nnorthwesterly winds and some clouds will prevent heat indices from \nreaching 110, they will most likely reach 105 in a wider area. As \nexpected, the hottest part of the region will be east of the Blue \nRidge where another Heat Advisory may be necessary. The warmth will \nstill persist into Friday but the ridge will begin to displace \nwestward and storm activity may prevent temperatures from\nreaching as high as Thursday. A cold front is expected to \narrive by Saturday, which will temporarily bring air \ntemperatures and dew points down a little.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Multiple chance of showers and thunderstorms\nThursday through this week weekend.\n\nSome high- res guidance has convection upstream over PA this\nmorning shifting/developing southward into northern VA this\nafternoon, and could see it reaching a Buckingham to South\nBoston/Charlotte Court House area and points east by late\nafternoon. Ridge building from the southwest to suppress\nconvection for the most part elsewhere.\n\nPrevious discussion...\n\n\nA small side note before getting into the weekend storms: As \nthe mid-level ridge continues to approach our forecast \nterritory, ridge rider storm systems will be traveling around \nthe 500mb high and deliver rain and thunderstorms to northern \nand eastern Virginia this afternoon and Thursday. These storms \nhave a chance to graze the central Piedmont of Virginia but \nconfidence is low given they will have to battle the suppressive\nprestige of the ridge.\n\nA cold front on Friday starts to make its way southwestward from the \nnortheast. Around the same time the center of the ridge starts to \nshow cracks in its structure. The ridge will eventually be \npushed off westward and reorganize over the Great Plains as the \ncold front pushes through. Another system traveling along the \ninner rings of the ridge is likely to intercept our area and \ndeliver thunderstorms, some which may be severe. The better \nkinematics so far look to be over the Piedmont of Virginia and \nthrough the Alleghany Highlands. The cold front now looks to \narrive by Saturday instead of Friday, meaning the better \nnorthwesterly mean flow will be delayed until Saturday, leading \nto weaker lapse rates for Friday's storms. Still, CAPE values of\n1000-2000 J/kg and effective wind shear of 25-35 knots will \nprovide enough storm organization for damaging wind gusts.\n\nThe cold front's passage will provide another round of showers and \nthunderstorms on Saturday. Additional shortwaves on Saturday and \nSunday will likely keep the precipitation chances going into early \nnext week.",
